      "file": "items/prop-qftx-categorified-braid-euler.md",
      "item_id": "prop-qftx-categorified-braid-euler",
      "status": "repair",
      "strategy": "direct",
      "proposed_proof": "**Given:** $n\\ge2$, a braid $\\beta\\in B_n$, and the Rouquier complexes and grading convention of [[def-qftx-categorified-line-model]].\n\n[F1] [[prop-decategorification-of-a-rouquier-complex-is-the-hecke-braid-generator]]: in the stated shift convention, $\\Phi(\\chi(F_i))=vT_i$ and $\\Phi(\\chi(F_i^{-1}))=v^{-1}T_i^{-1}$ for each simple generator.\n\n[F2] [[lem-euler-class-of-a-rouquier-complex-is-homotopy-invariant-and-multiplicative]]: $\\chi$ is invariant under homotopy equivalence and satisfies $\\chi(C\\otimes_R D)=\\chi(C)\\chi(D)$ for the signed tensor totalization of bounded complexes with terms in $\\mathrm{SBim}_n$.\n\n[F3] [[thm-rouquier-complex-is-well-defined-up-to-canonical-homotopy-equivalence]]: signed words representing the same braid determine canonically homotopy-equivalent Rouquier complexes.\n\n[F4] [[def-braid-group-by-the-artin-presentation]]: $B_n$ is generated by $\\sigma_1,\\ldots,\\sigma_{n-1}$ with adjacent braid relations and distant commutation relations; inserting or deleting an inverse pair is also a word equality.\n\n[F5] [[def-complex-homotopy-and-contractibility-in-an-additive-category]]: a cochain complex is contractible when there are maps $h^m:C^m\\to C^{m-1}$ satisfying $1_{C^m}=d^{m-1}h^m+h^{m+1}d^m$ for every $m$.\n\n[F6] [[def-qftx-categorified-line-model]]: a signed word $\\sigma=\\sigma_{i_1}^{\\epsilon_1}\\cdots\\sigma_{i_r}^{\\epsilon_r}$ is assigned the signed tensor totalization $F(\\sigma)=F_{i_1}^{\\epsilon_1}\\otimes_R\\cdots\\otimes_R F_{i_r}^{\\epsilon_r}$, and its braid has this complex up to canonical homotopy equivalence.\n\n[A1] Use $T_\\beta$ for the standard homomorphism image specified in the Statement; no physical interpretation of $v$ is assumed.\n\n\n\n1.1 Choose a signed word $\\sigma=\\sigma_{i_1}^{\\epsilon_1}\\cdots\\sigma_{i_r}^{\\epsilon_r}$ representing $\\beta$. By the word definition and multiplicativity in [F2], $\\chi(F(\\sigma))=\\prod_{k=1}^r\\chi(F_{i_k}^{\\epsilon_k})$. Applying the algebra homomorphism $\\Phi$ and the generator values in [F1] gives $\\Phi(\\chi(F(\\sigma)))=v^{\\sum_k\\epsilon_k}T_{i_1}^{\\epsilon_1}\\cdots T_{i_r}^{\\epsilon_r}$. [A1, F1, F2, F6, construct]\n\n2.1 The exponent sum is unchanged by each defining relation: the two sides of an adjacent braid relation each have sum $3$, the two sides of a distant commutation relation each have sum $2$, and an inverse pair has sum $0$. Thus $e(\\sigma)$ descends to $e(\\beta)\\in\\mathbb Z$ and satisfies $e(\\beta\\gamma)=e(\\beta)+e(\\gamma)$. The product of the $T_i^{\\epsilon_k}$ is $T_\\beta$ by the specified group homomorphism, so step 1.1 gives the displayed formula independent of the chosen word. [F4, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 The comparison in [F3] identifies $F(\\beta\\gamma)$ in the homotopy category with the signed tensor totalization $F(\\beta)\\otimes_R F(\\gamma)$. Applying homotopy invariance and multiplicativity from [F2], then applying the algebra homomorphism $\\Phi$, gives $\\Phi(\\chi(F(\\beta\\gamma)))=\\Phi(\\chi(F(\\beta)))\\Phi(\\chi(F(\\gamma)))$. Dividing by $v^{e(\\beta\\gamma)}=v^{e(\\beta)}v^{e(\\gamma)}$ proves multiplicativity of the normalized map. [F2, F3, step 2.1, algebra]\n\n3.2 For a positive generator the formula is $vT_i$ and for its inverse it is $v^{-1}T_i^{-1}$ by [F1]. Since $v$ is an indeterminate in the Laurent polynomial coefficient ring, these factors cannot be discarded for nonzero exponent sum. The normalized value is exactly $T_\\beta$ by step 2.1. [F1, step 2.1, algebra]\n\n4.1 Let $D$ be the two-term complex with $D^0=D^1=R$, differential $d^0=1_R$, and all other terms zero. The map $h^1=1_R:D^1\\to D^0$ (all other $h^m=0$) satisfies the contracting-homotopy equation in [F5], so $D$ is homotopy equivalent to the zero complex. But its graded terms are $R$ in degrees $0$ and $1$, whereas the zero complex has zero terms; since $R=\\mathbb Q[x_1,\\ldots,x_n]$ has $1\\ne0$, the complexes are not isomorphic as graded complexes. Their Euler classes are both zero by [F2] and $[R]-[R]=0$. [F2, F5, F6, step 1.1] ∎"
    },
